  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce detailed technical tutorial. Need final only. Need ensure complete sentences, avoid repetition. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows, examples tradeoffs pitfalls.\n\nPlan structure:\n- Intro: SQL query execution stages.\n- Example database schema and query. Use concrete: employees, departments, orders? Let's pick: employees(id PK, department_id FK, name, salary), departments(id PK, name), salary_band? Query: SELECT e.name, d.name, e.salary FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON e.department_id = d.id WHERE e.salary > 100000 AND d.name = 'Engineering' ORDER BY e.salary DESC LIMIT 10;\nNeed explain:\n1 Parsing and validation: lexer, parser to AST, semantic analysis, privileges.\n2 Query rewrite/simplification: constant folding, predicate pushdown, common subexpression, decorrelation.\n3 Statistics and optimizer: cost-based, cardinality estimates, histograms, selectivity. Plan generation: choose scan, index, join order, join algorithm, aggregation, sort. Dynamic vs static.\n4 Physical plan/execution engine: Volcano/pipeline/iterator, pull model, vectorized.\n5 Indexes: B-tree, composite, covering, partial, hash, GiST? In general RDBMS B-tree. Explain how index used: point lookup, range, join, sort. Tradeoffs write amplification, space, maintenance. Examples indexes: departments(name) unique? employees(salary) or composite (department_id, salary) maybe. For query need d.name='Engineering' and e.salary>100000. Best index maybe employees(department_id, salary) if we first find department id, or employees(salary, department_id) if range on salary first. Need explain optimizer may choose. Composite index left-prefix. If filter d.name = 'Engineering', need departments index. If department Engineering has many employees, better scan employees by department and filter salary? Index employees(department_id, salary) can seek to dept and range salary. If many Engineering, maybe. If salary range selective, index employees(salary, department_id) could scan salary range and lookup dept?",
